Title
Project Slingstone: A Lunar Electromagnetic Mass Driver for Cislunar Logistics — Mathematical Foundations, Orbital Mechanics, and Electromagnetic Propulsion Architecture

Author
Ouadi Maakoul

Abstract
This dissertation develops the complete physical and mathematical blueprint for a lunar‑surface electromagnetic mass driver capable of launching standardised 200 kg payloads to cislunar transfer orbits at exit velocities of 2.38 km s⁻¹. The system accelerates the combined 500 kg sled‑and‑payload assembly at a sustained 20 g (196.2 m s⁻²) over a 15.9 km track, achieving a launch cadence of 12 per Earth‑day. All derivations rest on first‑principles mechanics, electromagnetism, and orbital dynamics, rigorously validated against contemporary experimental literature. Key contributions include: (i) a closed‑form proof that the Shackleton south‑pole site cannot exploit passive mascon‑resonant orbits, necessitating active capture for all trajectories; (ii) the first complete subsystem‑by‑subsystem efficiency chain for a linear synchronous motor with Inductrack levitation, yielding an overall electrical‑to‑kinetic efficiency of 81.5 %; (iii) an exact Inductrack lift‑to‑drag derivation showing that levitation drag is negligible (L/D ≫ 10³ at exit speed); (iv) a predictive feed‑forward terminal guidance architecture resolving the inadequacy of pure feedback control; (v) a thorough energy‑storage analysis demonstrating that an all‑solid‑state battery bank buffered by a small capacitor bank can deliver the required 1.74 GJ per launch, integrated with a shared 40 kW(e) fission surface power reactor for eclipse resilience. Nine critical gaps identified in an external review are closed, and the architecture is shown to be physically realisable with current or near‑term technology extensions. The corrected energy chain, the quench‑protection design for REBCO coils, the structural foundation anchorage, and the radiator sizing are all explicitly resolved. The annual throughput of 876 t yr⁻¹ establishes the Moon as a viable logistics hub for deep‑space exploration.

