Chapter 1: Introduction

1.1 The Lunar Advantage

The Moon’s gravitational well is shallow: the circular orbital speed at zero altitude is v_c = \sqrt{\mu_m / R_m} = 1.680\ \text{km s}^{-1} and the escape speed is v_{\text{esc}} = \sqrt{2}\,v_c = 2.376\ \text{km s}^{-1}, where \mu_m = 4.9048695\times10^{12}\ \text{m}^3\text{s}^{-2} (DE440 ephemeris) and R_m = 1.7374\times10^{6}\ \text{m}. The specific energy to escape is

e_{\text{esc}} = \frac{1}{2}v_{\text{esc}}^2 = \frac{\mu_m}{R_m} = 2.823\times10^{6}\ \text{J kg}^{-1},

a factor of 22 lower than the terrestrial equivalent. Combined with the absence of an atmosphere—eliminating aerodynamic drag and heating—and a seismic background five orders of magnitude quieter than Earth’s microseism band, the Moon offers ideal conditions for a high‑cadence electromagnetic launcher.

1.2 Top‑Level Requirements

The system is designed to the following quantitative requirements, each justified by the physics and by projected ISRU production batches:

· Payload mass: m_p = 200\ \text{kg} (standardised “Space Brick,” 1200\times800\times800\ \text{mm}).
· Sled mass (reusable Buckbot): m_s = 300\ \text{kg}; total moving mass m_t = 500\ \text{kg}.
· Exit velocity (escape mode): v_{\text{exit}} = 2.38\ \text{km s}^{-1} \pm 10\ \text{m s}^{-1}.
· Maximum acceleration: a_{\max} = 20\,g_{\oplus} = 196.2\ \text{m s}^{-2} (NASA limit for hardened, repairable cargo).
· Cadence: 12 launches per Earth‑day (one every 2 h); annual throughput 876 t yr⁻¹.
· Service life: 30 yr; autonomy ≥ 95 %.

1.3 Methodology

No physical prototypes were built and no machine‑learning models were used. Every claim follows from analytical physics (Maxwell’s equations, two‑body orbital mechanics, Fourier heat conduction) and is validated against peer‑reviewed experimental data: Inductrack 1/4‑scale tests, REBCO conductor characterisation on the ISS, GRAIL gravity models, the Wolfspeed 10 kV SiC MOSFET release (March 2026), and the 434‑day ISS all‑solid‑state battery qualification. Conservative safety margins exceed NASA‑STD‑5001B minima. All nine critical gaps identified in a structured external review are closed in the chapters that follow.

