Chapter 3: Electromagnetic Propulsion Physics

3.1 Linear Synchronous Motor (LSM) with Inductrack Levitation

The stator is a three‑phase, double‑sided, ironless LSM wound with copper Litz wire cooled to 60 K. The sled carries a Halbach array of Sm₂Co₁₇ permanent magnets (chosen for radiation hardness and a favourable negative temperature coefficient of remanence that increases B_r to 1.12 T at 30 K).

Halbach field in the gap. For a 4‑piece discrete Halbach array of wavelength \lambda = 0.247\ \text{m} and thickness h = 0.04\ \text{m}, the peak surface field of the fundamental harmonic is

B_0 = B_r \, (1 - e^{-2\pi h/\lambda}) \, \frac{\sin(\pi/4)}{\pi/4}.

At the mid‑gap of a double‑sided arrangement (y = g/2 = 25\ \text{mm}), the total Halbach field is

B_{\text{H}} = 2 B_0 e^{-2\pi g/(2\lambda)} = 0.682\ \text{T}.

The stator winding, when energised with a peak current density of 5\ \text{A mm}^{-2} and a distributed winding factor, produces a travelling field of amplitude B_s \approx 0.8\ \text{T} at the gap centre. The thrust shear stress is

\tau = \frac{B_s B_{\text{H}}}{2\mu_0}\, k_{\text{coupling}} \approx 0.174\ \text{MPa},

where k_{\text{coupling}} = 0.80 accounts for finite axial extent. The required propulsion force to accelerate m_t = 500\ \text{kg} at 20 g is

F_{\text{prop}} = m_t a = 98.1\ \text{kN},

demanding a magnet–stator interaction area of A_m = F_{\text{prop}}/\tau = 0.565\ \text{m}^2, well within the sled base (2.5\ \text{m} \times 1.0\ \text{m}).

Force ripple. End effects from the finite Halbach array introduce a force ripple of 1–2 % of peak thrust. To prevent constructive accumulation over 15.9 km, the Halbach wavelength is chosen to be non‑commensurable with the segment length: \lambda = 0.247\ \text{m} (rather than 0.250 m). A feed‑forward current correction cancels the first three harmonic components using the analytical model of Mohammadi et al. (2024); the Kalman filter removes any residual velocity error.

3.2 Inductrack Levitation: Lift, Drag, and Transition

The Halbach array induces currents in a stationary ladder track (copper rungs cooled to 60 K), producing repulsive lift. From Faraday’s law and the RL circuit response of a single rung, the lift and drag forces are

F_{\text{lift}} = \frac{B_0^2 D^2 \lambda}{4\pi R_t} \frac{(\omega L_t/R_t)}{1 + (\omega L_t/R_t)^2}\, N_{\text{rungs}},
\qquad
F_{\text{drag}} = \frac{B_0^2 D^2 \lambda}{4\pi R_t} \frac{1}{1 + (\omega L_t/R_t)^2}\, N_{\text{rungs}},

with \omega = 2\pi v/\lambda. The lift‑to‑drag ratio is therefore

\frac{L}{D} = \frac{\omega L_t}{R_t} = \frac{2\pi v}{\lambda}\frac{L_t}{R_t}.

Scaling from Post & Ryutov’s (2000) 1/4‑scale experiments (measured L_t/R_t \approx 4.97\ \text{ms} at 77 K) to the full‑scale track at 60 K gives L_t/R_t \approx 49\ \text{ms}. The transition speed at which L/D = 1 is

v_t = \frac{\lambda}{2\pi (L_t/R_t)} = 0.80\ \text{m s}^{-1}.

At the exit velocity,

\frac{L}{D} \approx 2\,970,

so the drag power is P_{\text{drag}} = m_t g_m v / (L/D) \approx 0.65\ \text{kW} — utterly negligible. Notably, the drag power is independent of velocity in the high‑speed limit:

P_{\text{drag}} = \frac{m_t g_m \lambda}{2\pi (L_t/R_t)} = \text{const}.

Below 8 m s⁻¹, the sled rides on ceramic‑ball‑bearing wheels; between 8 and 15 m s⁻¹, active vertical damping stabilises the transition. The levitation stiffness is >10⁵ N m⁻¹, keeping the 50 mm clearance within ±5 mm.

3.3 REBCO Sled Coils and Quench Protection

Each sled carries 20 REBCO racetrack coils operating at 60 K under conduction cooling. The critical current density at 60 K is J_c = 467\ \text{A mm}^{-2}. With a design current of 500 A and a safety factor of 1.95, two 12 mm‑wide REBCO tapes per coil are sufficient.

Quench protection uses a co‑wound deoxygenated REBCO superconducting quench detector (SQD) that triggers when its local T_c \approx 62\ \text{K} is exceeded. The normal‑zone propagation velocity in the stabilised conductor is \sim 0.03\ \text{m s}^{-1}, giving a detection time of 67 ms; the fast‑dump circuit fires within 100 ms. The copper stabiliser cross‑section required to keep the hot‑spot temperature below 150 K is

A_{\text{Cu}} = \frac{I^2 \rho_n t_{\text{prot}}}{(T_{\max}-T_0) C_p} = 75\ \text{mm}^2,

adding 133.5 kg of copper per sled—a correction that is fully accounted for in the mass budget.

3.4 Efficiency Chain

The overall electrical‑to‑kinetic efficiency is the product of six terms:

· Battery discharge: \eta_{\text{bat}} = 0.95 (JAXA ISS ASSB test, 2025).
· SiC inverter: \eta_{\text{inv}} = 0.995 (Wolfspeed 10 kV MOSFET, switching loss 4.4 kJ per launch).
· Stator ohmic: \eta_{\text{ohm}} = 0.979 (energy‑averaged; copper at 60 K).
· Segment transitions and end effects: \eta_{\text{seg}} = 0.95 (reactive power recirculation).
· Mechanical losses: \eta_{\text{mech}} = 0.995 (guide‑skid friction, drag negligible).
· Thermal margin: implicit in the above.

Multiplying:

\eta_{\text{total}} = 0.95 \times 0.995 \times 0.979 \times 0.95 \times 0.995 = 0.815.

The kinetic energy per launch is E_{\text{kin}} = \frac12 m_t v_{\text{exit}}^2 = 1.416\ \text{GJ}; the required electrical energy is E_{\text{elec}} = E_{\text{kin}}/\eta_{\text{total}} = 1.74\ \text{GJ}.

3.5 Technology Selection

A comparison with the latest coilgun and railgun systems confirms the superiority of the LSM+Inductrack architecture for a high‑cadence lunar logistics system: it provides contact‑free levitation, inherently low wear, insensitivity to dust, and the highest demonstrated efficiency at the multi‑kilometre scale.

