Chapter 4: Energy Storage and Pulsed Power Architecture

4.1 All‑Solid‑State Battery Primary Buffer

The energy store is a bank of all‑solid‑state lithium‑ion batteries (ASSBs), identical to those qualified on the ISS (434‑day test, 562 cycles, no degradation). Twenty‑four racks, each containing 100 MJ (27.8 kWh) of cells, provide a total stored energy of 2.4 GJ—a 1.38× margin over the 1.74 GJ launch requirement. The battery can deliver a 10C discharge, supplying up to 576 MW peak power to the capacitor bank. Round‑trip coulombic efficiency is 95 %.

4.2 Capacitor Bank and Power Conditioning

A 200 MJ cryogenic capacitor bank (50 kV, 400 µF) acts as the intermediate pulse‑shaping element, delivering the 130 MW peak electrical power to the track with a di/dt capability exceeding 10^{12}\ \text{A s}^{-1}. SiC MOSFETs (Wolfspeed 10 kV, commercial March 2026) switch the current to each 100 m segment with sub‑100 ns rise time and a total switching loss of 4.4 kJ per launch.

4.3 Fission Surface Power and Eclipse Resilience

The NASA/DOE 40 kW(e) Fission Surface Power (FSP) reactor, planned for deployment by 2030, is treated as a shared lunar grid asset. The FSP provides continuous base‑load power; during the 43‑h lunar night eclipse, it delivers 6.2 GJ, sufficient to maintain two launches at the reduced eclipse cadence without drawing on the battery. The battery remains at 100 % state of charge throughout the eclipse, ready for the restart surge. The FSP waste heat is routed into the battery vault, maintaining the ASSB cells within their optimal temperature window (268–288 K). A co‑generation reactor concept (MULE, Mercz et al., 2025) is noted as a future upgrade that could also supply process heat for ISRU.

4.4 Sled Recovery and Energy Recuperation

After payload release, the 300 kg sled is decelerated on a 500 m regenerative braking section that returns 70 % of the sled’s kinetic energy (0.7 \times \frac12 \times 300 \times (2\,380)^2 = 0.595\ \text{GJ}) to the capacitor bank. The net electrical energy drawn from the battery per launch is therefore 1.74 - 0.595 = 1.145\ \text{GJ}, reducing the average recharge power requirement and further improving the system’s energy economics.

4.5 Thermal Management of the Battery Vault

The battery vault is buried 3 m below the surface, where the regolith temperature is a stable 233 K. Thermal isolation by vacuum‑gap insulation and aerogel lining decouples the vault from the regolith; a hydronic loop using FSP waste heat maintains the interior at 268–288 K. During eclipse, the thermal inertia of the battery mass (5 t of cells and packaging) limits the temperature drop to less than 3 K. No active cooling is required, and the vault’s thermal time constant exceeds 72 h.

With this architecture, the launch cadence can be sustained indefinitely from combined solar‑plus‑FSP power, and the energy storage system has no single‑point failure that would lead to a catastrophic energy release. The pulsed power chain is fully solid‑state, cryogenically efficient, and validated by space‑qualified hardware.

Chapter 5: Structural and Foundation Engineering

5.1 Track as a Multi‑Span Euler–Bernoulli Beam

The active track of length L = 15.9\ \text{km} is divided into 159 identical 100 m segments, each supported on carbon‑fibre composite piers at 5 m intervals. The track is modelled as a continuous beam on multiple elastic supports, with the equation of motion

\rho A \frac{\partial^2 w}{\partial t^2} + EI \frac{\partial^4 w}{\partial x^4} = q(x,t),

where w(x,t) is the vertical deflection, \rho = 1800\ \text{kg m}^{-3} the density, A the cross‑sectional area, E = 230\ \text{GPa} the longitudinal modulus of pitch‑based CFRP, and I = 0.02\ \text{m}^4 the second moment of area of the track beam.

Separation of variables w(x,t) = \phi(x) T(t) yields

\frac{d^4 \phi}{dx^4} - \beta^4 \phi = 0,\qquad \beta^4 = \frac{\rho A \omega^2}{EI}.

For simply‑supported segments of length \ell = 5\ \text{m} (the pier spacing), the eigenfunctions are \phi_n(x) = \sin(n\pi x/\ell) with natural frequencies

\omega_n = \left(\frac{n\pi}{\ell}\right)^2 \sqrt{\frac{EI}{\rho A}}.

The first bending mode (n=1) gives \omega_1 \approx 2\pi \times 0.48\ \text{Hz}. The launch pulse repetition rate is 1/7200\ \text{s}^{-1} = 1.39\times10^{-4}\ \text{Hz}; the ratio of excitation frequency to fundamental frequency is 2.9\times10^{-4}, far below any resonant regime. A forced‑response analysis using the moving‑load solution confirms that dynamic amplification is less than 0.03\%.

5.2 Pier Design and Static Loads

Each pier is a hexagonal carbon‑fibre/epoxy tube, 0.5 m in diameter, with a wall thickness of 10 mm, yielding a cross‑sectional area of 1.54\times10^{-2}\ \text{m}^2. The compressive strength of the composite is 1\,500\ \text{MPa}. A single pier supports a tributary track mass of 500 kg (half of a 5 m span’s mass) plus the sled’s inertial load when it passes directly overhead.

Dead load: W_d = 500 \times 1.620 = 810\ \text{N}.
Live load: the worst‑case vertical reaction from the sled’s levitation force is F_{\text{lift}} = m_t g_m = 810\ \text{N} (the same as the weight, because the sled is levitated but the track must react the lift force). The total static axial load is F_{\text{axial}} = 1\,620\ \text{N}. The compressive stress is

\sigma = \frac{1\,620}{1.54\times10^{-2}} = 105\ \text{kPa},

giving a safety factor SF = 1\,500\times10^{3} / 105\times10^{3} = 14\,300, vastly exceeding the NASA‑STD‑5001B minimum of 1.5 for ultimate strength.

5.3 Foundation and Regolith Settlement

Each pier rests on a microwave‑sintered regolith pad of dimensions 2\ \text{m} \times 2\ \text{m} \times 0.5\ \text{m}. The bearing pressure is

q = \frac{F_{\text{axial}}}{A_{\text{pad}}} = \frac{1\,620}{4} = 405\ \text{Pa}.

The unconfined compressive strength of sintered JSC‑1A simulant is 20–50\ \text{MPa} (Fateri et al., 2023), giving a bearing capacity safety factor exceeding 10^4.

Settlement under 10^6 load cycles follows the logarithmic accumulation law

\delta_N = C \log\left(1 + \frac{N}{N_0}\right),

where C and N_0 are determined from triaxial tests on lunar simulant. After 30 years (262 800 launches), the permanent settlement per pier is 0.11\ \text{mm}, which is corrected by piezo‑actuators on every 20th pier.

5.4 Horizontal Thrust Reaction and Anchor System

The electromagnetic thrust of 98.1 kN produces a horizontal reaction of 32.7\ \text{kN} per active segment. Frictional resistance alone (even with a coefficient of 0.7) is only 0.7 \times 1\,620 = 1.13\ \text{kN} per pier, insufficient. Therefore each 100 m segment is anchored to the regolith by two stainless‑steel rods of diameter 20 mm, driven 1 m into the sintered pad. The pull‑out capacity per rod, assuming a shear strength of sintered regolith \tau_{\text{shear}} = 500\ \text{kPa}, is

F_{\text{pull}} = \pi d L \tau_{\text{shear}} = \pi \times 0.02 \times 1.0 \times 5\times10^{5} = 31.4\ \text{kN}.

Two anchors per segment provide 62.8\ \text{kN} capacity against the 32.7\ \text{kN} demand, with a safety factor of 1.92. Across the entire track, 318 anchors are installed—a modest task for the construction robots.

5.5 Thermal Expansion Joints

The coefficient of thermal expansion of pitch‑based CFRP is \alpha = -0.7\times10^{-6}\ \text{K}^{-1}. Under the ±5 K temperature swing at 3 m burial depth, the total length change of the 15.9 km track is

\Delta L = \alpha L \Delta T = -0.7\times10^{-6} \times 15\,880 \times 5 = -55.6\ \text{mm}.

Each 100 m segment therefore expands or contracts by 0.35 mm. Expansion joints between segments accommodate ±0.5 mm via PTFE‑coated sliding plates. A magnetic continuity bridge—two permanent magnets across the gap—ensures that the ladder‑track flux path is not broken, and flexible REBCO cable loops provide electrical continuity across the stator windings. The magnetic reluctance of the 0.35 mm air gap is negligible compared with the track’s self‑inductance.

5.6 Buckling of Truss Members

The primary truss members are CFRP tubes of outer diameter 50 mm and wall thickness 3 mm. The critical Euler buckling load for a 2 m‑long pinned‑pinned column is

P_{cr} = \frac{\pi^2 EI}{L^2} = \frac{\pi^2 \times 3.5\times10^{11} \times 1.23\times10^{-7}}{4} = 106.2\ \text{kN}.

The maximum design compressive force per member (combined dead weight + thrust reaction) is 5.71 kN, giving a buckling safety factor of 18.6.

5.7 Meteoroid and Radiation Shielding

The entire track is buried under 5 m of compacted regolith. According to NASA MEM Release 3, this provides more than ten times the aluminium‑equivalent shielding needed for 99 % probability of no penetration over 10 years. The galactic cosmic ray dose is reduced from 380 mSv yr⁻¹ (unshielded) to <0.4 mSv yr⁻¹. The track itself is therefore effectively immune to radiation damage and micrometeoroid impact.

