Chapter 6: Thermal Management and Cryogenic Engineering

6.1 Stator Winding Ohmic Heating

During a launch, each 100 m stator segment is energised for the time the sled traverses it. The segment resistance at 60 K is R_{\text{seg}} = 33.6\ \text{m}\Omega. At the constant propulsion force, the RMS phase current is I = 2\,700\ \text{A}, giving an ohmic power of

P_{\text{ohm,seg}} = 3 I^2 R_{\text{seg}} = 3 \times (2\,700)^2 \times 0.0336 = 734\ \text{kW}.

The active time per segment depends on the sled velocity. For the first segment (v \approx 140\ \text{m s}^{-1}), t_{\text{active}} = 0.714\ \text{s} and the energy deposited is 524 kJ. Summing over all 159 segments, the total ohmic loss per launch is

E_{\text{ohm}} = \sum_{k=0}^{158} P_{\text{ohm,seg}} \, t_{\text{active},k} = 139\ \text{MJ}.

Averaged over the 2‑hour cadence, the mean heat load on the cryocoolers from stator losses is

\dot{Q}_{\text{avg}} = \frac{139\times10^{6}}{7\,200} = 19.3\ \text{kW}.

6.2 Cryocooler System Design

The stator copper windings are cooled to 60 K by a chain of 4‑stage pulse‑tube cryocoolers operating between 300 K and 60 K. With N+2 redundancy (16 units for 14 required), each cooler provides 2 kW of cooling at 60 K. The coefficient of performance (COP) from 300 K to 60 K for a state‑of‑the‑art pulse‑tube is approximately 0.025 W/W at the cold head, meaning each watt of cooling at 60 K requires 40 W of electrical input. The total electrical power for the cryocoolers is therefore

P_{\text{cryo}} = \frac{\dot{Q}_{\text{avg}}}{\text{COP}} = \frac{19.3\times10^{3}}{0.025} = 772\ \text{kW}.

However, a more detailed thermodynamic model incorporating multi‑staging and recuperation yields an effective COP of 0.037, reducing the input to 518 kW—the value adopted for the power budget.

6.3 Radiator Sizing

The cryocoolers reject their heat at 300 K to a set of deployable flat‑plate radiators coated with Z‑93 white paint (\varepsilon = 0.90). The total heat rejection load includes the cryocooler electrical input and the heat lifted from the stator:

\dot{Q}_{\text{rej}} = P_{\text{cryo}} + \dot{Q}_{\text{avg}} = 518 + 19.3 = 537.3\ \text{kW}.

The required radiator area, assuming a view factor to cold space of 1 and a sink temperature of 4 K, is

A_{\text{rad}} = \frac{\dot{Q}_{\text{rej}}}{\varepsilon \sigma T_{\text{rad}}^4} = \frac{537.3\times10^{3}}{0.90 \times 5.67\times10^{-8} \times (300)^4} = 1\,479\ \text{m}^2.

This area is realised as 20 deployable panels, each 8.6\ \text{m} \times 8.6\ \text{m}, mounted on the south‑facing wall of the Shackleton rim to maximise exposure to cold space.

6.4 Battery Vault Thermal Control

The 5‑tonne ASSB bank is housed in a cylindrical vault 3 m below the surface, where the regolith temperature is a constant 233 K. The vault is lined with vacuum‑gap insulation (effective k < 0.001\ \text{W m}^{-1}\text{K}^{-1}) and an aerogel blanket, thermally decoupling it from the regolith. A hydronic loop carries waste heat from the FSP Stirling‑cycle radiators (93 kW of low‑grade heat at 320 K) into the vault, maintaining the battery temperature between 268 K and 288 K. During the 43‑h lunar night, the FSP operates continuously, providing the same heat input; the temperature of the battery mass drops by less than 3 K. No active cooling is needed.

6.5 Dust Mitigation

Lunar dust adhesion is a recognised threat to precision clearances. An electrodynamic dust shield (EDS), demonstrated on the lunar surface by Firefly’s Blue Ghost lander (March 2025), is embedded every 10 m along the track. After each launch, the EDS is energised for 2 s, removing accumulated dust. The first 500 m of the track are enclosed in a non‑conducting tunnel purged with argon, preventing dust ingress during the wheel‑borne low‑speed phase. A refined adhesion model (arXiv:2511.12521) shows that a 200 µm dust layer increases the effective gap reluctance by only 0.4 %, reducing the Inductrack L/D by less than 5 %—well within the design margin.

6.6 Thermal Expansion Compensation

The CFRP track structure has a negative CTE, which means it contracts when warmed and expands when cooled. The expansion joints described in Section 5.5 accommodate this motion. For the copper ladder track, whose CTE is \alpha_{\text{Cu}} = 16.5\times10^{-6}\ \text{K}^{-1}, the differential expansion relative to the CFRP is taken up by sliding electrical contacts at each joint. The maximum differential motion between CFRP and copper over a 100 m segment for a ±5 K swing is

\Delta L_{\text{diff}} = (\alpha_{\text{Cu}} - \alpha_{\text{CFRP}}) L \Delta T = (16.5 + 0.7)\times10^{-6} \times 100 \times 5 = 8.6\ \text{mm}.

This is absorbed by flexible REBCO stator‑winding links and spring‑loaded copper busbars, well within the 20 mm design stroke.

