Chapter 9: Conclusions, Contributions, and Path Forward

9.1 Summary of Mathematical and Physical Results

This dissertation has derived from first principles a complete, physically consistent architecture for a lunar electromagnetic mass driver. The fundamental findings are:

1. Orbital mechanics. The Shackleton south‑pole site cannot access equatorial mascon‑resonant orbits (Theorem 2.1); all payloads require active capture, and the capture reliability exceeds 0.998 with a 250 m s⁻¹ tug budget.
2. Track kinematics. A constant acceleration of 20\,g_{\oplus} over 15.9\ \text{km} achieves the required 2.38\ \text{km s}^{-1} exit velocity with a 10 % margin. The earlier published 15.9 km length was shown to be correct for the selected acceleration, resolving the earlier inconsistency.
3. Electromagnetic propulsion. The LSM + Inductrack combination yields an overall efficiency of 81.5 %. The Inductrack L/D ratio exceeds 2\,970 at exit velocity, making drag losses negligible.
4. Quench protection. The co‑wound REBCO SQD and copper stabiliser (75 mm²) ensure hotspot temperatures remain below 150 K, closing the quench safety gap.
5. Energy storage. The ASSB + capacitor architecture provides 1.74\ \text{GJ} per launch, with regenerative braking recovering 70 % of the sled kinetic energy. The system is integrated with a 40 kW(e) fission surface power reactor for eclipse resilience.
6. Structural and thermal. Regolith foundation settlement is limited to 0.11\ \text{mm} over 30 years; radiators of 1\,479\ \text{m}^2 maintain the 60 K stator temperature; and the buried track is immune to radiation and micrometeoroids.

9.2 Gap‑Closure Verification

All nine gaps from the May 2026 external review have been explicitly closed:

1. Dual‑mode mascon launch – Polar inaccessibility proven; equatorial option noted.
