Appendix B — Orbital Mechanics Proofs and Trajectory Analysis

B.1 Escape condition and track length

The specific mechanical energy of a test mass at distance r from the Moon’s centre with speed v is

\mathcal{E} = \frac{v^2}{2} - \frac{\mu_m}{r}. \tag{B.1}

Escape occurs for \mathcal{E} \ge 0; the minimum escape speed at the surface (r = R_m) is therefore

v_{\text{esc}} = \sqrt{\frac{2\mu_m}{R_m}} = 2\,376\ \text{m}\,\text{s}^{-1}. \tag{B.2}

With a design exit velocity v_{\text{exit}} = 2\,380\ \text{m}\,\text{s}^{-1} and a constant net acceleration a = 20\,g_{\oplus} = 196.2\ \text{m}\,\text{s}^{-2} along the track, the ideal track length follows from v_{\text{exit}}^2 = 2 a L_{\text{ideal}}:

L_{\text{ideal}} = \frac{(2\,380)^2}{2\times196.2} = 14\,436\ \text{m}. \tag{B.3}

A 10 % margin for control and jerk‑limited ramps gives the adopted baseline

L = 15.9\ \text{km}. \tag{B.4}

The gravity loss from the track elevation angle \varepsilon = 15^\circ is a_{\text{grav}} = g_m\sin\varepsilon = 0.419\ \text{m}\,\text{s}^{-2}, or 0.21\% of the thrust, and is absorbed within the margin.

B.2 Mascon‑assisted sub‑escape mode: polar inaccessibility proof

Janhunen (2024) showed that equatorial nearside sites can exploit lunar mascons to trap a payload launched at v \approx 1.70\ \text{km}\,\text{s}^{-1} into a 9‑day orbit. The required orbital inclination is near‑equatorial (i \approx 0^\circ).

For a launch from the Shackleton rim (\varphi = -89.9^\circ, \lambda = 0^\circ) with azimuth \beta = 85^\circ and elevation \varepsilon = 15^\circ, the inertial velocity vector in Moon‑centred coordinates is

\mathbf{v}_0 = v_{\text{exit}} \begin{pmatrix} 0.08470 \\ 0.9622 \\ -0.2589 \end{pmatrix},
\qquad \mathbf{r}_0 = R_m \begin{pmatrix} 0.001745 \\ 0 \\ -0.99998 \end{pmatrix}.

The specific angular momentum \mathbf{h} = \mathbf{r}_0\times\mathbf{v}_0 has magnitude |\mathbf{h}| = 3.994\times10^9\ \text{m}^2\,\text{s}^{-1} and its z‑component is h_z = 6.936\times10^6\ \text{m}^2\,\text{s}^{-1}. The orbit inclination is therefore

\cos i = \frac{h_z}{|\mathbf{h}|} = 1.737\times10^{-3} \quad\Longrightarrow\quad i = 89.90^\circ.

A plane‑change from 89.90^\circ to 0^\circ at circular speed v_{\text{circ}} \approx 1.63\ \text{km}\,\text{s}^{-1} would require

\Delta v_{\text{plane}} = 2 v_{\text{circ}} \sin\frac{\Delta i}{2} \approx 2\,270\ \text{m}\,\text{s}^{-1},

which exceeds the escape velocity itself. Hence the mascon sub‑escape mode is inaccessible from the south pole. The mascon mode is retained as a future option for an equatorial launch facility.

