Appendix C — Electromagnetic Propulsion Derivations

C.1 Halbach array field in the gap

An ideal Halbach array of wavelength \lambda and thickness h produces, at its surface, a sinusoidal field whose fundamental harmonic has peak amplitude

B_0 = B_r\left(1 - e^{-2\pi h/\lambda}\right)\frac{\sin(\pi/4)}{\pi/4}. \tag{C.1}

For a four‑piece discrete array, \sin(\pi/4)/(\pi/4) = 2\sqrt{2}/\pi \approx 0.9003. With B_r(30\ \text{K}) = 1.119\ \text{T}, \lambda = 0.247\ \text{m}, h = 0.040\ \text{m},

B_0 = 1.119 \times (1 - e^{-2\pi\times0.04/0.247}) \times 0.9003 = 0.644\ \text{T}.

The field decays exponentially with distance from the array. For a double‑sided arrangement with a total gap g = 50\ \text{mm} (each array 25\ \text{mm} from the mid‑plane), the field at the sled centre is

B_{\text{H}} = 2 B_0 e^{-2\pi(g/2)/\lambda} = 2 \times 0.644 \times e^{-\pi\times0.05/0.247} = 0.682\ \text{T}. \tag{C.2}

C.2 Thrust shear stress and propulsion force

The stator winding, when energised with a peak current density J = 5\ \text{A}\,\text{mm}^{-2} and a distributed winding factor k_w = 0.933, produces a travelling field of amplitude

B_s = \frac{\mu_0}{2} \frac{J A_{\text{slot}} k_w}{g_{\text{eff}}} \approx 0.8\ \text{T}

at the gap centre. The thrust shear stress is

\tau = \frac{B_s B_{\text{H}}}{2\mu_0}\, k_{\text{coupling}}, \tag{C.3}

where k_{\text{coupling}} = 0.80 accounts for finite axial extent and slotting effects. Substituting,

\tau = \frac{0.8 \times 0.682}{2\times4\pi\times10^{-7}} \times 0.80 = 0.174\ \text{MPa}.

The required propulsion force to accelerate m_t = 500\ \text{kg} at 20\,g_{\oplus} is

F_{\text{prop}} = m_t a = 500 \times 196.2 = 98.1\ \text{kN},

so the required interaction area is

A_m = \frac{F_{\text{prop}}}{\tau} = \frac{98.1\times10^3}{0.174\times10^6} = 0.565\ \text{m}^2,

well within the sled base area of 2.5\ \text{m} \times 1.0\ \text{m} = 2.5\ \text{m}^2.

C.3 Force ripple and feed‑forward cancellation

The finite Halbach array produces an end‑effect force ripple of amplitude ≈ 2 % of the mean thrust. To prevent constructive accumulation over the 159 segments, the Halbach wavelength is chosen as \lambda = 0.247\ \text{m} (not 0.250 m). Over one 100 m segment, the ripple phase advances by

\Delta\phi = 2\pi\left(\frac{100}{0.247} - \left\lfloor\frac{100}{0.247}\right\rfloor\right) \approx 0.823\ \text{rad}.

The cumulative phase over the whole track is 159\times0.823 \approx 130.9\ \text{rad}, guaranteeing near‑perfect cancellation of the fundamental ripple. Higher harmonics are suppressed by a feed‑forward current correction that injects the first three harmonic components derived from the analytical model of Mohammadi et al. (2024).

C.4 Overall efficiency chain

The electrical‑to‑kinetic efficiency is the product of the following terms, each validated against experimental data:

· Battery discharge (ASSB): \eta_{\text{bat}} = 0.95 (JAXA ISS test, 2025).
· SiC inverter (Wolfspeed 10 kV MOSFET): switching loss 4.4\ \text{kJ} per launch, \eta_{\text{inv}} = 0.995.
· Stator ohmic (energy‑averaged at 60 K): \eta_{\text{ohm}} = 0.979.
· Segment transitions and end effects: \eta_{\text{seg}} = 0.95.
· Mechanical (guide skids, drag): \eta_{\text{mech}} = 0.995.

Hence

\eta_{\text{total}} = 0.95 \times 0.995 \times 0.979 \times 0.95 \times 0.995 = 0.815.

The kinetic energy imparted to the moving mass is E_{\text{kin}} = \frac12 \times 500 \times (2\,380)^2 = 1.416\ \text{GJ}; the required electrical energy is

E_{\text{elec}} = \frac{E_{\text{kin}}}{\eta_{\text{total}}} = 1.74\ \text{GJ}. \tag{C.4}

