Appendix D — Inductrack Levitation and Drag Analysis

D.1 Lift and drag on a single rung

Consider a single shorted rectangular rung of width D, resistance R_t, and inductance L_t, moving with velocity v through the sinusoidal Halbach field of amplitude B_0. The flux through the rung is \Phi(t) = B_0 D \delta \sin(2\pi vt/\lambda), where \delta is the effective penetration depth. The induced EMF is \mathcal{E} = -d\Phi/dt, and the rung current satisfies

L_t\frac{dI}{dt} + R_t I = \mathcal{E}. \tag{D.1}

For sinusoidal excitation \mathcal{E} = \mathcal{E}_0\cos(\omega t) with \omega = 2\pi v/\lambda, the steady‑state amplitude is

I_0 = \frac{\mathcal{E}_0}{\sqrt{R_t^2 + \omega^2 L_t^2}}. \tag{D.2}

The lift and drag forces are obtained from the Lorentz force on the rung, integrated over the track:

F_{\text{lift}} = \frac{B_0^2 D^2 \lambda}{4\pi R_t} \frac{\omega L_t/R_t}{1 + (\omega L_t/R_t)^2} N_{\text{rungs}}, \tag{D.3}

F_{\text{drag}} = \frac{B_0^2 D^2 \lambda}{4\pi R_t} \frac{1}{1 + (\omega L_t/R_t)^2} N_{\text{rungs}}. \tag{D.4}

The lift‑to‑drag ratio is therefore

\frac{L}{D} \equiv \frac{F_{\text{lift}}}{F_{\text{drag}}} = \frac{\omega L_t}{R_t} = \frac{2\pi v}{\lambda}\frac{L_t}{R_t}. \tag{D.5}

Equation (D.5) shows that L/D grows linearly with velocity in the high‑speed regime, and that the drag power P_{\text{drag}} = F_{\text{drag}} v is independent of velocity when L/D \gg 1:

P_{\text{drag}} = \frac{m_t g_m \lambda}{2\pi(L_t/R_t)} = \text{const}. \tag{D.6}

D.2 Track time constant and transition speed

The rung resistance at 60 K is

R_t = \frac{\rho_{\text{Cu}} D}{A_{\text{rung}}} = \frac{2.8\times10^{-9}\times 1.0}{50\times10^{-6}} = 5.6\times10^{-5}\ \Omega.

The effective inductance per rung, accounting for mutual coupling in the ladder track, is taken from the transmission‑line model and validated against Post & Ryutov (2000). The measured ratio for a geometrically similar track at 77 K is L_t/R_t \approx 4.97\ \text{ms}; scaling to 60 K (\rho reduction factor 2.5) and to the full wavelength (\lambda = 0.247\ \text{m} vs. 0.0625 m) gives

\frac{L_t}{R_t}\bigg|_{\text{Slingstone}} \approx 4.97\ \text{ms} \times \frac{0.247}{0.0625} \times 2.5 = 49.1\ \text{ms}.

The transition speed at which L/D = 1 is

v_t = \frac{\lambda}{2\pi(L_t/R_t)} = \frac{0.247}{2\pi \times 49.1\times10^{-3}} = 0.80\ \text{m}\,\text{s}^{-1}. \tag{D.7}

At the exit velocity v_{\text{exit}} = 2\,380\ \text{m}\,\text{s}^{-1},

\frac{L}{D} = \frac{2\pi \times 2\,380 \times 49.1\times10^{-3}}{0.247} \approx 2\,970.

The drag power is therefore

P_{\text{drag}} = \frac{500 \times 1.620 \times 2\,380}{2\,970} \approx 650\ \text{W},

and the total drag energy loss over the 13.34 s transit is \approx 8.7\ \text{kJ}—completely negligible.

D.3 Low‑speed transition stability

Below 8 m s⁻¹, the sled rides on ceramic‑ball‑bearing wheels; the Halbach array produces insufficient lift. Between 8 and 15 m s⁻¹, the vertical motion is actively damped by modulating the stator phase currents to create a restoring force proportional to the vertical displacement. The linearised vertical dynamics are

m_t \ddot{z} + k_z z = u(t), \quad k_z \approx 10^5\ \text{N}\,\text{m}^{-1},

with a state‑feedback controller that places the closed‑loop poles at \omega_n = 15\ \text{rad}\,\text{s}^{-1} and \zeta = 0.7. The transition is completed smoothly, and the clearance is maintained at 50\ \text{mm} \pm 5\ \text{mm} for all speeds.

