Appendix E — Energy Storage and Pulsed Power Architecture

E.1 All‑solid‑state battery sizing

The ASSB bank uses cells with a specific energy of 350\ \text{Wh}\,\text{kg}^{-1} (cell level) and a 10 C continuous discharge capability. The required energy per launch is E_{\text{elec}} = 1.74\ \text{GJ}. With a 1.38× margin, the total stored energy is 2.40\ \text{GJ} = 667\ \text{kWh}. The cell mass required is

m_{\text{cells}} = \frac{667\ \text{kWh}}{0.350\ \text{kWh}\,\text{kg}^{-1}} = 1\,906\ \text{kg}.

Packaging, busbars, and battery management add 50 % overhead, bringing the total rack mass to 2 860 kg. The design uses 24 racks, each storing 100 MJ (27.8 kWh), for a total mass of 24 \times 159 = 3\,816\ \text{kg} (including conservative packaging margins). In the detailed mass budget, 26 racks are provisioned, allowing two spares.

E.2 Regenerative braking and net energy

After payload release, the 300 kg sled is decelerated from 2 380 m s⁻¹ to 10 m s⁻¹ on a 500 m regenerative braking section. The kinetic energy of the sled alone is

E_{\text{sled}} = \frac12 \times 300 \times (2\,380)^2 = 0.850\ \text{GJ}.

With a recovery efficiency \eta_{\text{regen}} = 0.70 (McNab, 2022), the energy returned to the capacitor bank is

E_{\text{recovered}} = 0.70 \times 0.850 = 0.595\ \text{GJ}.

The net electrical energy drawn from the battery per launch is therefore

E_{\text{net}} = 1.74 - 0.595 = 1.145\ \text{GJ}. \tag{E.1}

E.3 Eclipse power balance

The FSP reactor provides 40 kW(e) continuously. During the longest lunar night (43 h), it delivers

E_{\text{FSP}} = 40\times10^3 \times 43 \times 3\,600 = 6.192\ \text{GJ}.

The base load (cryocoolers, avionics, etc.) is 17 kW, consuming 17\times10^3 \times 43 \times 3\,600 = 2.63\ \text{GJ}. Two launches are planned during the eclipse, each drawing 1.145\ \text{GJ} net from the battery, but the FSP surplus of 6.192 - 2.63 = 3.56\ \text{GJ} is more than sufficient to cover the 2.29\ \text{GJ} launch energy. The battery remains at 100 % state‑of‑charge throughout.

