Appendix F — Thermal Management Derivations

F.1 Stator ohmic heating per segment

The ohmic power dissipated in one 100 m segment, energised with an RMS phase current of 2\,700\ \text{A} and a resistance of 33.6\ \text{m}\Omega, is

P_{\text{ohm,seg}} = 3 I^2 R = 3 \times (2\,700)^2 \times 0.0336 = 734\ \text{kW}.

The active time for segment k is t_k = 100\ \text{m} / v_k, where v_k is the sled velocity when it traverses that segment. Summing over all 159 segments yields the total ohmic loss

E_{\text{ohm}} = P_{\text{ohm,seg}} \sum_{k=0}^{158} t_k = 139\ \text{MJ},

consistent with the 8 % loss term in the efficiency chain.

F.2 Cryocooler power and radiator sizing

The average heat load on the cryogenic system is

\dot{Q}_{\text{avg}} = \frac{139\times10^6}{7\,200\ \text{s}} = 19.3\ \text{kW}.

With a practical COP of 0.037 for a multi‑stage pulse‑tube operating between 300 K and 60 K, the electrical input to the cryocoolers is

P_{\text{cryo}} = \frac{\dot{Q}_{\text{avg}}}{\text{COP}} = \frac{19.3}{0.037} = 522\ \text{kW}.

The total heat rejected at 300 K is

\dot{Q}_{\text{rej}} = P_{\text{cryo}} + \dot{Q}_{\text{avg}} = 522 + 19.3 = 541\ \text{kW}.

The required radiator area, assuming emissivity \varepsilon = 0.90 and a view factor to cold space of 1, is

A_{\text{rad}} = \frac{\dot{Q}_{\text{rej}}}{\varepsilon\sigma T_{\text{rad}}^4} = \frac{541\times10^{3}}{0.90 \times 5.67\times10^{-8} \times (300)^4} = 1\,479\ \text{m}^2. \tag{F.1}

F.3 Battery vault thermal model

The vault, buried 3 m deep, is thermally isolated from the regolith by vacuum‑gap insulation. The regolith temperature at that depth is a constant 233 K. Waste heat from the FSP radiators (93 kW at 320 K) is circulated through the vault via a hydronic loop, maintaining the battery temperature between 268 K and 288 K. During the 43 h eclipse, the FSP continues to supply heat, and the temperature drop of the 5 t battery mass is

\Delta T = \frac{Q_{\text{loss}} \Delta t}{m c_p} \approx \frac{(2\,630 \times 43\times3\,600)}{5\,000 \times 840} \approx 2.5\ \text{K},

well within the operational limits.

