Appendix G — Structural and Foundation Engineering Proofs

G.1 Euler–Bernoulli beam modal analysis

For a uniform beam on simple supports spaced at \ell = 5\ \text{m}, the eigenvalue problem is

EI \frac{d^4\phi_n}{dx^4} - \rho A \omega_n^2 \phi_n = 0, \qquad \phi_n(0) = \phi_n(\ell) = 0,\quad \phi_n''(0) = \phi_n''(\ell) = 0.

The solutions are \phi_n(x) = \sin(n\pi x/\ell) with natural frequencies

\omega_n = \left(\frac{n\pi}{\ell}\right)^2 \sqrt{\frac{EI}{\rho A}}.

Using E = 230\ \text{GPa}, I = 0.02\ \text{m}^4, \rho = 1\,800\ \text{kg}\,\text{m}^{-3}, and A = 0.05\ \text{m}^2, the first bending mode is

\omega_1 = \left(\frac{\pi}{5}\right)^2 \sqrt{\frac{230\times10^9 \times 0.02}{1\,800 \times 0.05}} \approx 0.48\ \text{Hz}.

The launch repetition rate is 1/7\,200\ \text{Hz} = 1.39\times10^{-4}\ \text{Hz}; the frequency ratio is 2.9\times10^{-4}, far below any resonant amplification.

G.2 Foundation bearing and anchor design

The static vertical load per pier is F_{\text{axial}} = 1\,620\ \text{N}. The sintered regolith bearing pad has an area 4\ \text{m}^2 and an unconfined compressive strength of 20\ \text{MPa}, giving a bearing safety factor > 10⁴.

The electromagnetic thrust of 98.1 kN produces a horizontal reaction of 32.7 kN per active segment. Friction alone provides only 0.7 \times 1\,620 = 1.13\ \text{kN} per pier, so each 100 m segment is anchored by two 20 mm‑diameter steel rods driven 1 m into the sintered pad. The pull‑out capacity per rod, assuming a shear strength of 500 kPa, is

F_{\text{pull}} = \pi d L \tau_{\text{shear}} = \pi \times 0.02 \times 1.0 \times 5\times10^{5} = 31.4\ \text{kN}.

Two rods provide 62.8 kN, giving a safety factor of 1.92 against the design load.

G.3 Regolith settlement

Under 10^6 load cycles, the permanent settlement per pier follows the logarithmic law

\delta_N = C \log\left(1 + \frac{N}{N_0}\right),

with C = 0.05\ \text{mm} and N_0 = 10^4 derived from JSC‑1A triaxial tests. For N = 262\,800 launches,

\delta_{30\,\text{yr}} = 0.05 \log\left(1 + 26.28\right) = 0.11\ \text{mm},

which is corrected by piezo‑actuators on every 20th pier.

G.4 Thermal expansion joints

The CFRP track structure has a CTE \alpha_{\text{CFRP}} = -0.7\times10^{-6}\ \text{K}^{-1}. Over a 100 m segment and a \pm5\ \text{K} temperature swing, the length change is

\Delta L = \alpha L \Delta T = -0.7\times10^{-6} \times 100 \times 5 = 0.35\ \text{mm}.

Expansion joints accommodate \pm0.5\ \text{mm} via PTFE‑coated sliding plates. The differential expansion between the copper ladder track (\alpha_{\text{Cu}} = 16.5\times10^{-6}\ \text{K}^{-1}) and the CFRP is

\Delta L_{\text{diff}} = (16.5 + 0.7)\times10^{-6} \times 100 \times 5 = 8.6\ \text{mm},

absorbed by flexible REBCO stator‑winding links and spring‑loaded copper busbars with a 20 mm stroke.

