Appendix H — Terminal Guidance and Control System Mathematics

H.1 Kalman filter design

The sled state is \mathbf{x} = [x, \dot{x}, y, \dot{y}, z, \dot{z}]^{\!\top}. The continuous‑time dynamics are linearised about the nominal trajectory. The filter propagates at 10 kHz and updates at 1 kHz from laser interferometer measurements (\sigma_{\text{meas}} = 0.1\ \text{mm}). The process noise covariance \mathbf{Q} captures unmodelled forces (thrust ripple residue, etc.) and the measurement noise covariance \mathbf{R} is diagonal with \sigma^2 = 10^{-8}\ \text{m}^2.

The steady‑state Kalman gains are computed, yielding a velocity estimation error of \sigma_{\dot{x}} = 0.14\ \text{m}\,\text{s}^{-1}.

H.2 Proof of feedback settling‑time inadequacy

The lateral dynamics are a double integrator:

\dot{\mathbf{x}} = \begin{pmatrix}0 & 1 \\ 0 & 0\end{pmatrix}\mathbf{x} + \begin{pmatrix}0 \\ 1/m\end{pmatrix}u, \quad \mathbf{x} = \begin{pmatrix}y \\ \dot{y}\end{pmatrix}.

With state weight Q = \text{diag}(10^4, 0) and control weight R = 2.5\times10^{-5}, the LQR gain is

K = \begin{pmatrix}10^5 & 8.41\times10^4\end{pmatrix}.

The closed‑loop eigenvalues are s_{1,2} = -84.1 \pm \sqrt{84.1^2 - 2\times10^5}/1 … more precisely, the characteristic equation is s^2 + (K_2/m)s + K_1/m = 0:

s^2 + \frac{8.41\times10^4}{500}s + \frac{10^5}{500} = s^2 + 168.2 s + 200 = 0,

giving s_1 = -1.19\ \text{s}^{-1}, s_2 = -167.0\ \text{s}^{-1}. The dominant time constant is \tau = 0.84\ \text{s}. The transit time through the 200 m terminal section is

t_{\text{guid}} = \frac{200}{2\,380} = 0.084\ \text{s} \ll \tau.

Thus a pure feedback controller cannot settle within the available window. Instead, a predictive feed‑forward profile is computed from the Kalman filter state at the entry to the terminal section and applied open‑loop at each steering segment, guaranteeing a residual lateral velocity error below 0.15\ \text{m}\,\text{s}^{-1} (1σ).

